From 1e44a60330adf9d91efd5ac6e8f554cb33660ba8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 04:05:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/8] koblitz_index_calculus: scan keys formed from the rests'
 slopes

In a normal basis squaring is a rotation by one and the basis change is
linear, so a rest's abscissa x3 = l^2 + l + xP + xQ + a has coordinates
c + rotl(c) + d + e, with c = coords(l), d = coords(xQ) and
e = coords(xP + a).  A prepared scan now computes each rest's slope only
(`FastCurve::slopes_many`), keys it from c (`slope_keys_paced`), with d
kept per base point on the table, and builds the whole rest from its slope
only for the keys the filter admits.  That spares the squaring, the
abscissa and its store for every rejected summand.  The slopes' batched
inversion runs eight chains (`Gf2::batch_inv8`), the same inverses.

A test checks, at four degrees and with degenerate sums mixed in, that the
slopes are add_many_lazy's, the slope keys are the rests' keys, and a sum
rebuilt from its slope is the sum.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013vyAr6TGoMhy56CQmvaUCd
